opening_hook\\\": \\\"How resilient is your supply chain? See how digital transformation is making manufacturers more adaptive, connected, and competitive in a volatile world.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\",\"refusal\":null,\"annotations\":[]},\"logprobs\":null,\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"}],\"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":2250,\"completion_tokens\":1888,\"total_tokens\":4138,\"prompt_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":1920,\"audio_tokens\":0},\"completion_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":0,\"audio_tokens\":0,\"accepted_prediction_tokens\":0,\"rejected_prediction_tokens\":0}},\"service_tier\":\"default\",\"system_fingerprint\":\"fp_1a2c4a5ede\"}',NULL,NULL,'1',2250,1888,79140,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,1,'planner-synopses-a38c0b3c-9f4b-4b47-a1bc-e03e2380cd57-b4-1769685','2026-01-29 11:11:49','2026-01-29 11:12:11'),('61760af4-9fe9-46e9-a901-5037a8c66c4c','a38c0b3c-9f4b-4b47-a1bc-e03e2380cd57',NULL,'gpt-4.1','completed','You are the Author Agent. Write a draft article based on the provided framework.\nReturn ONLY valid JSON.\nSchema: {\"title\":\"\",\"draft\":\"\",\"key_points\":[\"\"],\"citations\":[{\"title\":\"\",\"url\":\"\",\"quote\":\"\"}]}\nArticle headline: AI-Driven Automation in Manufacturing: How Smart Factories Will Transform 2024’s Industrial Landscape\nSynopsis: Explore how manufacturers are leveraging AI, robotics, and digital twins to drive efficiency, quality, and operational resilience. This article provides a data-backed look at adoption trends, real-world use cases, and the near-future of smart manufacturing in a post-pandemic economy.\nKeywords: AI-driven automation, smart manufacturing, digital twins, industrial robots, factory efficiency, manufacturing transformation\nFramework:\n{\"title\":\"Editorial Framework: AI-Driven Automation in Manufacturing (Smart Factories) \\u2014 2024 Playbook\",\"overview\":\"This framework structures a 1,500\\u20132,500 word article explaining how AI, robotics, and digital twins are moving smart factories from pilots to measurable operational impact. It emphasizes what is changing in 2024: faster scaling, workflow redesign, and tighter governance, cybersecurity, and workforce enablement. The goal is to connect technology choices to concrete plant-floor outcomes (throughput, quality, uptime, and resilience).\",\"context\":\"Manufacturers are accelerating smart manufacturing programs as supply volatility and margin pressure reward higher productivity, agility, and operational transparency. Recent surveys show broad conviction that smart manufacturing will drive competitiveness, but many initiatives still face common barriers: talent gaps, complex transformations, and operational risk. Editorially, the opportunity is to translate the \\u201csmart factory\\u201d concept into an actionable roadmap with examples and metrics readers can use to benchmark progress.\",\"application\":{\"intended_reader\":\"Manufacturing executives, plant leaders, operations\\/OT leaders, and digital transformation program owners.\",\"use_case\":\"Use this framework to outline a practical, evidence-led article that prioritizes high-value use cases (quality, throughput, maintenance, and planning), clarifies enabling capabilities (data foundation, digital twins, robotics, and AI), and provides implementation guardrails (cybersecurity, governance, and workforce upskilling).\"},\"observations\":[{\"headline\":\"Smart manufacturing is shifting from promise to value realization.\",\"detail\":\"Large-scale survey evidence indicates manufacturers increasingly view smart manufacturing as a competitiveness driver over the near term. Reported impacts cluster around output, productivity, and capacity gains rather than abstract \\u201cdigital transformation\\u201d narratives. The article should foreground specific operational KPIs and show how they are achieved (instrumentation, analytics, and process changes), not just the technologies involved.\"},{\"headline\":\"Scaling is still the hard part; workflow redesign is a differentiator.\",\"detail\":\"Across industries, many organizations remain stuck in experimentation or piloting, even as AI use becomes common. The strongest results correlate with redesigning workflows and embedding AI into day-to-day decision loops, not simply deploying models. Position 2024 as the year manufacturers move from isolated pilots (e.g., one line) to repeatable patterns (multi-site playbooks).\"},{\"headline\":\"Digital twins and AI converge as the operating model for the modern plant.\",\"detail\":\"Digital twins are most compelling when paired with AI for prediction, optimization, and closed-loop control\\u2014especially in bottleneck management, quality, and asset performance. The narrative should explain how twins connect engineering, operations, and maintenance via a common data layer. Include guidance on where to start (one asset\\/line, then expand) and what \\u201cdone\\u201d looks like (validated models, governed data, and measurable KPI lift).\"},{\"headline\":\"Robotics adoption is being reshaped by AI, autonomy, and orchestration.\",\"detail\":\"Robots are increasingly integrated into broader automation platforms where AI coordinates tasks, exceptions, and real-time work allocation. This changes vendor decisions and architecture: manufacturers must plan for interoperability, orchestration, and process intelligence rather than single-point automations. Emphasize selection criteria that reduce integration risk (open interfaces, OT compatibility, and lifecycle support).\"},{\"headline\":\"Cybersecurity, governance, and skills are now first-order constraints.\",\"detail\":\"As plants connect more assets and deploy more autonomous capabilities, operational risk expands and requires stronger controls, oversight models, and cyber investment. Multiple reports highlight that governance maturity can lag technology adoption, especially for more autonomous systems. The article should provide a practical checklist: governance roles, model monitoring, OT security controls, and workforce upskilling plans.\"}],\"key_themes\":[\"From pilots to scale: repeatable use-case factories and multi-site rollout patterns.\",\"Digital twins + AI for predictive and prescriptive operations.\",\"Robotics and automation orchestration as a platform strategy.\",\"Operational KPIs: throughput, quality, OEE, capacity unlock, and downtime reduction.\",\"Risk management: cybersecurity, governance, and safe autonomy.\",\"Workforce enablement: upskilling, human-in-the-loop operations, and change management.\"]}\nCitations:\n[{\"apa\":\"Gaus, T., & Schlotterbeck, M. (2025, May 1). 2025 Smart Manufacturing and Operations Survey: Navigating challenges to implementation. Deloitte Insights.\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.deloitte.com\\/us\\/en\\/insights\\/industry\\/manufacturing\\/2025-smart-manufacturing-survey.html\",\"relevance\":\"Provides manufacturing-specific survey data on perceived competitiveness drivers and reported improvements (e.g., output, productivity, and capacity), plus common implementation challenges such as talent and operational risk.\"},{\"apa\":\"Singla, A., Sukharevsky, A., Hall, B., Yee, L., & Chui, M. (2025, November 5). The state of AI in 2025: Agents, innovation, and transformation. McKinsey & Company.\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.mckinsey.com\\/capabilities\\/quantumblack\\/our-insights\\/the-state-of-ai\",\"relevance\":\"Documents that AI use is widespread but scaling remains limited, and highlights workflow redesign as a key success factor. Useful to frame why manufacturers must move beyond pilots to capture enterprise-level value.\"},{\"apa\":\"Deloitte. (2026). The State of AI in the Enterprise: Deloitte\\u2019s 2026 AI report tracking adoption and impact. Deloitte AI Institute.\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.deloitte.com\\/us\\/en\\/what-we-do\\/capabilities\\/applied-artificial-intelligence\\/content\\/state-of-ai-in-the-enterprise.html\",\"relevance\":\"Adds enterprise AI adoption context, including access expansion, governance gaps for agentic systems, and the primacy of AI fluency\\/skills development\\u2014directly supporting the article\\u2019s governance and workforce sections.\"},{\"apa\":\"Gartner. (n.d.). Smart factory trends for manufacturing industries. Gartner.\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.gartner.com\\/en\\/supply-chain\\/trends\\/smart-factory-trends\",\"relevance\":\"Defines smart factory operations as a strategic trend and frames practical questions leaders ask: measuring value, core technologies used, lessons learned, and success factors. Useful for structuring the article into decision-oriented sections.\"},{\"apa\":\"Le Clair, C., & Schaffrik, B. (2024, February 23). AI is reshaping automation markets: A guide to process tool evolution. Forrester.\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.forrester.com\\/report\\/ai-is-reshaping-automation-markets\\/RES180584\",\"relevance\":\"Supports the platformization theme: consolidation of automation tool markets and a shift toward \\u201cautomation fabric\\u201d concepts. This helps explain why orchestration and integrated process intelligence matter for manufacturing automation strategies.\"}]','{\"id\":\"chatcmpl-D3UXDzxp7D6Wa0SaCvqNl2sKyysup\",\"object\":\"chat.completion\",\"created\":1769724731,\"model\":\"gpt-4.1-2025-04-14\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"{\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"AI-Driven Automation in Manufacturing: How Smart Factories Will Transform 2024\\u2019s Industrial Landscape\\\",\\n  \\\"draft\\\": \\\"As the world enters a new phase of digital transformation, manufacturers are radically accelerating smart factory initiatives to meet the challenges of supply volatility and ongoing economic uncertainty. In 2024, AI, advanced robotics, and digital twins are no longer just buzzwords but foundational technologies charting the course for measurable improvements in efficiency, quality, and resilience.\\\\n\\\\n## From Promise to Value: The New Reality of Smart Manufacturing\\\\n\\\\nRecent industry survey data demonstrates a marked shift in executive sentiment: smart manufacturing is now seen as a front-line driver of competitiveness. According to Deloitte\\u2019s 2025 Smart Manufacturing and Operations Survey, more than 80% of manufacturers indicated that smart initiatives had a measurable positive impact on output, productivity, and capacity (Gaus & Schlotterbeck, 2025). The days of abstract \\u201cdigital transformation\\u201d rhetoric are behind us; the expectation is concrete operational KPIs\\u2014throughput, downtime reduction, and quality\\u2014all tied to precise technological investments.\\\\n\\\\n## Breaking Out of Pilot Purgatory: Scaling Demands Workflow Redesign\\\\n\\\\nDespite the momentum, the majority of smart factory programs still struggle to move beyond pilot phases. As highlighted in McKinsey\\u2019s 2025 state of AI report, widespread adoption of AI has not yet translated into scaled, repeatable improvements across enterprises (Singla et al., 2025). The differentiator is not model deployment, but rather integrating AI into everyday decision loops and redesigning workflows around new digital capabilities. Forward-thinking manufacturers in 2024 are establishing cross-site playbooks, transforming isolated pilots into enterprise-wide value.\\\\n\\\\n## Digital Twins and AI: The Converging Operating System of the Plant\\\\n\\\\nDigital twins\\u2014virtual replicas of physical assets or processes\\u2014are evolving when coupled with AI to deliver predictive analytics and prescriptive recommendations. This synergy enables closed-loop control, especially for bottleneck management, asset reliability, and real-time quality assurance. Leaders are starting with high-impact assets or lines, validating their models with trusted data, and then expanding via governed multi-site frameworks. The future-ready \\u201csmart factory\\u201d is built upon a unified data layer seamlessly connecting engineering, operations, and maintenance, yielding measurable gains in Overall Equipment Effectiveness (OEE) and capacity.\\\\n\\\\n## Robotics: From Point Solutions to Orchestrated Automation Platforms\\\\n\\\\nAI-powered robotics is transforming from isolated automations to fully orchestrated platforms where autonomy, real-time coordination, and intelligent work allocation are strategic imperatives. As Forrester notes, this trend is fueling the rise of \'automation fabric\' architectures: interoperable, open-interface systems that integrate deeply with OT environments (Le Clair & Schaffrik, 2024). Vendor selection now hinges on reducing integration risks, embracing compatibility, and ensuring lifecycle support.\\\\n\\\\n## Guardrails First: Cybersecurity, Governance, and Workforce Enablement\\\\n\\\\nAs more plant assets become digitally enabled and autonomy increases, so too does operational risk. Survey results and research from the Deloitte AI Institute underscore that cybersecurity controls, rigorous governance models (such as OT\\/IT role clarity and model monitoring), and active upskilling programs are now nonnegotiable (Deloitte, 2026). Governance should be established before scaled deployments: practical checklists include formalizing cyber defenses, clarifying decision authorities, monitoring AI models for drift or bias, and ensuring operators are trained for digital and hybrid-manual oversight.\\\\n\\\\n## Practical Roadmap: High-Value Use Cases and Implementation Guardrails\\\\n\\\\nFor executives moving forward in 2024, the highest-return use cases remain in quality monitoring (predicting and preventing defects), throughput optimization (adaptive scheduling and real-time bottleneck management), predictive maintenance (AI-driven asset reliability programs), and dynamic resource planning. Leaders should prioritize building a robust data foundation; piloting with digital twins for closed-loop outcomes; planning automation with an orchestration-first mindset; and embedding cybersecurity and upskilling at every stage.\\\\n\\\\n## Conclusion: From Hype to Hard Numbers\\\\n\\\\nThe imperative is clear: smart factories represent not just technological advancement, but a path to operational resilience, agility, and sustainable competitiveness. As these capabilities move from experimentation to standard operating procedure, manufacturers must focus as much on implementation and governance as on innovation itself. 2024 is the year when smart manufacturing delivers on its promise\\u2014by scaling use cases, transforming workflows, governing risk, and investing in human capital.\\\",\\n  \\\"key_points\\\": [\\n    \\\"Manufacturers now see AI-driven automation and smart factories as critical to competitiveness, with measurable KPI improvements (output, capacity, and quality).\\\",\\n    \\\"Scaling successful pilots requires not just deploying AI, but redesigning workflows and decision processes for real impact.\\\",\\n    \\\"Digital twins, paired with AI, are transforming plant operations by enabling predictive and prescriptive capabilities, especially for bottleneck and asset management.\\\",\\n    \\\"Robotics adoption is being shaped by orchestration and platform strategies, emphasizing interoperability and lifecycle integration.\\\",\\n    \\\"Cybersecurity, governance, and workforce upskilling are essential implementation guardrails, not afterthoughts.\\\",\\n    \\\"A practical roadmap focuses on high-value use cases\\u2014quality, throughput, maintenance, and planning\\u2014supported by robust data and operational discipline.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"citations\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"2025 Smart Manufacturing and Operations Survey: Navigating challenges to implementation\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/www.deloitte.com\\/us\\/en\\/insights\\/industry\\/manufacturing\\/2025-smart-manufacturing-survey.html\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"More than 80% of manufacturers indicated that smart initiatives had a measurable positive impact on output, productivity, and capacity.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"The state of AI in 2025: Agents, innovation, and transformation\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/www.mckinsey.com\\/capabilities\\/quantumblack\\/our-insights\\/the-state-of-ai\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Widespread adoption of AI has not yet translated into scaled, repeatable improvements across enterprises; workflow redesign remains a key success factor.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"The State of AI in the Enterprise: Deloitte\\u2019s 2026 AI report tracking adoption and impact\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/www.deloitte.com\\/us\\/en\\/what-we-do\\/capabilities\\/applied-artificial-intelligence\\/content\\/state-of-ai-in-the-enterprise.html\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Cybersecurity controls, rigorous governance models, and active upskilling programs are now nonnegotiable as AI adoption expands in industrial contexts.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Smart factory trends for manufacturing industries\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/www.gartner.com\\/en\\/supply-chain\\/trends\\/smart-factory-trends\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Defines smart factory operations as a strategic trend and frames practical questions leaders ask: measuring value, core technologies used, lessons learned, and success factors.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"AI is reshaping automation markets: A guide to process tool evolution\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/www.forrester.com\\/report\\/ai-is-reshaping-automation-markets\\/RES180584\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"The shift toward \'automation fabric\' concepts highlights why orchestration and integrated process intelligence are central to manufacturing automation strategies.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\",\"refusal\":null,\"annotations\":[]},\"logprobs\":null,\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"}],\"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":1846,\"completion_tokens\":1509,\"total_tokens\":3355,\"prompt_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":1792,\"audio_tokens\":0},\"completion_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":0,\"audio_tokens\":0,\"accepted_prediction_tokens\":0,\"rejected_prediction_tokens\":0}},\"service_tier\":\"default\",\"system_fingerprint\":\"fp_1a2c4a5ede\"}',NULL,NULL,'1',1846,1509,63729,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,1,'planner-author-d12abca8-83b584c8-1769724730','2026-01-29 22:12:10','2026-01-29 22:12:44'),('631ac6ea-97aa-4bff-81a6-90565342a594','a38c0b3c-9f4b-4b47-a1bc-e03e2380cd57',NULL,'gpt-4.1','completed','You are the Author Agent. Write a draft article based on the provided framework.\nReturn ONLY valid JSON.\nSchema: {\"title\":\"\",\"draft\":\"\",\"key_points\":[\"\"],\"citations\":[{\"title\":\"\",\"url\":\"\",\"quote\":\"\"}]}\nArticle headline: Digital Twins and AI: Quality Breakthroughs for Smart Manufacturers in 2024\nSynopsis: This piece dives into the integration of digital twins and AI, examining how they drive unprecedented quality control and predictive performance in modern manufacturing facilities.\nKeywords: digital twins, smart factories, AI quality control, manufacturing simulation, predictive analytics, advanced robotics\nFramework:\n{\"title\":\"Digital Twins and AI: Quality Breakthroughs for Smart Manufacturers in 2024\",\"overview\":\"This editorial framework explains how digital twins and AI combine to improve manufacturing quality outcomes through real-time monitoring, predictive analytics, and prescriptive decision support. It prioritizes practical implementation patterns, quality-centric use cases, and data\\/architecture requirements. The structure supports a 1,500\\u20132,500 word article aimed at operations leaders evaluating where to start and how to scale.\",\"context\":\"Smart manufacturing programs are moving from experimentation to measurable value, but quality remains constrained by limited visibility, siloed data, and slow root-cause resolution. Digital twins provide a continuously updated virtual representation of products, assets, and factory systems, enabling simulation and \\u201cwhat-if\\u201d analysis without disrupting production. Adding AI shifts twins from passive models toward prescriptive, self-optimizing systems that can detect anomalies, predict failures, and recommend optimal process settings when data fidelity and system alignment are strong.\",\"application\":{\"intended_reader\":\"Manufacturing executives, plant managers, quality leaders, and Industry 4.0 program owners.\",\"use_case\":\"Plan and prioritize a 2024 roadmap for deploying AI-enabled digital twins to improve quality control, reduce defects, and increase throughput with clear data, integration, and change-management requirements.\"},\"observations\":[{\"headline\":\"AI turns digital twins into prescriptive quality systems.\",\"detail\":\"Digital twins bridge physical and virtual operations to enable real-time monitoring and predictive analytics, but AI expands them into autonomous, decision-capable systems. Recent manufacturing literature describes an evolution from passive simulation to prescriptive, self-optimizing twins that can optimize scheduling, control variables, and enhance quality control. For editorial planning, emphasize that quality gains come from closed-loop learning and action, not visualization alone.\"},{\"headline\":\"Use-case clarity matters because \\u201cdigital twin\\u201d spans multiple scopes.\",\"detail\":\"Digital twins can be built at product, asset, factory, or end-to-end supply chain scope, and each scope answers different operational questions. Product twins support defect root-cause analysis and warranty analytics, while asset and factory twins enable yield\\/throughput optimization and dynamic scheduling. The article should guide readers to choose the twin scope based on the specific quality constraint (e.g., defect mechanisms vs. line bottlenecks).\"},{\"headline\":\"Value is measurable when twins integrate operational data and decision workflows.\",\"detail\":\"Factory twins can compress decision cycles by simulating \\u201cwhat-if\\u201d scenarios from real-time conditions and linking outputs to scheduling and execution. Documented examples include cost savings from schedule redesign and reinforcement learning agents trained using the twin to improve sequencing outcomes compared with manual approaches. For quality-focused narratives, translate \\u201coptimization\\u201d into concrete metrics like scrap reduction, FPY improvement, and faster containment actions.\"},{\"headline\":\"Data fidelity and alignment are the gating constraints for quality breakthroughs.\",\"detail\":\"AI-enabled digital twins depend on high-fidelity, real-time data and a tight correspondence between the physical system and its digital counterpart. Manufacturing data is often fragmented across PLCs, sensors, MES, ERP, and human inputs, creating gaps that reduce model reliability and trust. The editorial plan should include a \\u201creadiness checklist\\u201d covering instrumentation, master data, data quality, and governance before promising advanced quality automation.\"},{\"headline\":\"Smart factory adoption is rising, but organizations must manage risk and talent gaps.\",\"detail\":\"Survey research indicates broad belief that smart manufacturing is becoming central to competitiveness, with reported average improvements in output, productivity, and capacity after implementation. However, implementation barriers commonly include complex transformation management, operational risk, cybersecurity needs, and workforce upskilling requirements. The article should balance opportunity with practical execution guidance: roles, operating model, and controls needed to sustain quality improvements at scale.\"}],\"key_themes\":[\"Closed-loop quality management with AI-enabled digital twins\",\"Choosing the right twin scope: product vs. asset vs. factory vs. end-to-end\",\"Real-time data integration across PLC, IoT, MES, and ERP for quality visibility\",\"Predictive and prescriptive analytics for defect prevention and process optimization\",\"Implementation readiness: data fidelity, governance, cybersecurity, and skills\"]}\nCitations:\n[{\"apa\":\"Nguyen, P., Kim, M., Nichols, E., & Yoon, H.-S. (2025). AI-driven digital twins for manufacturing: A review across hierarchical manufacturing system levels. Sensors, 26(1), 124. https:\\/\\/doi.org\\/10.3390\\/s26010124\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\\/articles\\/PMC12787485\\/\",\"relevance\":\"Provides a current synthesis of how AI integration transforms digital twins into prescriptive systems used for predictive maintenance, process optimization, quality control, and scheduling, while emphasizing data fidelity and alignment requirements.\"},{\"apa\":\"Camarella, S., Conway, M. P., Goering, K., & Huntington, M. (2024, January 10). Digital twins: The next frontier of factory optimization. McKinsey & Company. https:\\/\\/www.mckinsey.com\\/capabilities\\/operations\\/our-insights\\/digital-twins-the-next-frontier-of-factory-optimization\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.mckinsey.com\\/capabilities\\/operations\\/our-insights\\/digital-twins-the-next-frontier-of-factory-optimization\",\"relevance\":\"Defines digital twin scopes (product, asset, factory, end-to-end) and explains how factory twins support real-time decision-making, what-if simulation, and measurable operational improvements relevant to quality and throughput.\"},{\"apa\":\"Gaus, T., & Schlotterbeck, M. (2025, May 1). 2025 Smart Manufacturing and Operations Survey: Navigating challenges to implementation. Deloitte Insights. https:\\/\\/www.deloitte.com\\/us\\/en\\/insights\\/industry\\/manufacturing\\/2025-smart-manufacturing-survey.html\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.deloitte.com\\/us\\/en\\/insights\\/industry\\/manufacturing\\/2025-smart-manufacturing-survey.html\",\"relevance\":\"Offers executive survey evidence on smart manufacturing value realization and adoption barriers, including transformation complexity, operational risk, cybersecurity investments, and workforce upskilling needs.\"},{\"apa\":\"Gartner. (n.d.). Smart factory trends for manufacturing industries. Gartner. https:\\/\\/www.gartner.com\\/en\\/supply-chain\\/trends\\/smart-factory-trends\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.gartner.com\\/en\\/supply-chain\\/trends\\/smart-factory-trends\",\"relevance\":\"Frames smart factory operations as a strategic technology trend and highlights the need to measure value, select enabling technologies, learn from implementations, and define success practices that can anchor editorial guidance.\"}]','{\"id\":\"chatcmpl-D3fdkXFyjZNQFFYcXiu0rjdwK1lxr\",\"object\":\"chat.completion\",\"created\":1769767420,\"model\":\"gpt-4.1-2025-04-14\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"{\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Digital Twins and AI: Quality Breakthroughs for Smart Manufacturers in 2024\\\",\\n  \\\"draft\\\": \\\"In 2024, the convergence of digital twins and artificial intelligence (AI) is redefining quality control for smart manufacturers striving for competitive advantage. As digital transformation matures across the sector, manufacturers face persistent challenges: constrained visibility, siloed data, and slow, reactive approaches to defect resolution. This editorial explores how digital twins\\u2014dynamic, virtual representations of products, assets, or whole factories\\u2014combined with advanced AI, deliver breakthrough capabilities for quality-driven organizations seeking to scale measurable value.\\\\n\\\\n## The New Premise: Beyond Visualization to Prescriptive Quality\\\\nSmart factories were initially envisioned as highly visual, remotely monitored operations. Digital twins enhanced this vision by mapping physical systems to digital models, enabling simulation without disrupting production. However, this approach alone is insufficient for modern quality expectations. Recent advances see AI turning digital twins from passive observers into active, prescriptive systems. As AI models learn from real-time data, they adapt process controls, detect anomalies, and even prescribe corrective actions\\u2014accelerating root-cause resolution and defect prevention.\\\\n\\\\nResearch underscores this evolution. AI-enabled twins can self-optimize processes, improve quality control, and create closed-loop learning systems that shorten quality cycles (Nguyen et al., 2025). This marks a shift from using digital twins as monitoring dashboards to deploying them as autonomous, decision-capable assets integral to daily operations.\\\\n\\\\n## Twin Scope Matters: Aligning Digital Twin Implementation with Quality Constraints\\\\nA challenge for manufacturing leaders is where to start, since \'digital twin\' is an umbrella term spanning product-level twins for defect analytics, asset- and factory-level twins for throughput optimization, and even end-to-end supply chain twins. The correct scope depends on the quality constraint at hand. For example:\\\\n- **Product twins** facilitate fast root-cause analysis and warranty prediction, isolating issues down to batch or serial number.\\\\n- **Asset and line twins** focus on yield, downtime, and dynamic line balancing\\u2014optimal for environments with variable equipment performance.\\\\n- **Factory-wide twins** enable simulation of scheduling options, material flows, and process changes, supporting cross-departmental quality improvement.\\\\n\\\\nPractical advice: leaders should match digital twin scope to the specific operational question, maximizing investment impact by targeting the most constraining quality factors (Camarella et al., 2024).\\\\n\\\\n## Value Through Data Integration and Closed-Loop Analytics\\\\nWhat distinguishes high-performing digital twins is seamless data integration and actionable simulation. World-class factories collect real-time operational data from programmable logic controllers (PLCs), IoT sensors, MES, ERP, and human interfaces, feeding digital twins with high-fidelity inputs. This enables manufacturers to run \'what-if\' scenarios based on current factory conditions, optimizing process settings or triggering containment actions as soon as deviations are detected.\\\\n\\\\nRecent use cases highlight cost and quality wins: reinforcement learning agents, trained in a twin environment, outperform human sequencing; dynamic scheduling via twin simulation reduces scrap and elevates first-pass yield (FPY). Value is best measured by concrete metrics: reduced rework, higher FPY, and faster, more precise containment of emerging defects (Camarella et al., 2024).\\\\n\\\\n## Implementation Realities: Data, Governance, and Organizational Readiness\\\\nYet, the promise of AI-powered digital twins is gated by real-world constraints. Foremost is data fidelity: lack of standardized, real-time data between the factory floor and digital architecture erodes model trust and effectiveness. Manufacturers should conduct readiness checks focusing on instrumentation, data quality, master data alignment, and governance protocols before embarking on ambitious automation initiatives (Nguyen et al., 2025).\\\\n\\\\nMoreover, implementation is not only technological. Smart manufacturing surveys identify transformation complexity, cybersecurity, and workforce upskilling as consistent adoption barriers (Gaus & Schlotterbeck, 2025). Operations executives must design new organizational roles (e.g., digital twin product owners, data stewards), invest in cross-disciplinary upskilling, and establish cyber-physical security measures to sustain gains.\\\\n\\\\n## Building a Roadmap: From Pilot to Scale\\\\nSmart manufacturing adoption is at an inflection point. Executive surveys show measurable value in output, productivity, and quality from well-executed digital twin initiatives (Gaus & Schlotterbeck, 2025; Gartner, n.d.). However, failure to manage integration risk or address talent gaps can stall or reverse returns. To move from pilot to scale, manufacturing leaders should:\\\\n- Start with high-value, quality-constrained use cases and the corresponding digital twin scope.\\\\n- Ensure robust data and system integration; prioritize data quality and governance.\\\\n- Invest in AI tools for predictive and prescriptive analytics, emphasizing process optimization and defect prevention.\\\\n- Prepare the organization: upskill teams, redesign operating models, and enhance cybersecurity frameworks.\\\\n- Measure outcomes by concrete, quality-centric KPIs\\u2014scrap reduction, FPY improvement, and time-to-containment.\\\\n\\\\n## Conclusion\\\\nDigital twins infused with AI are redefining quality management for manufacturers bold enough to invest in data, talent, and governance. The advances in 2024 point toward a future where factories are not merely visualized, but continuously optimized\\u2014where every process, asset, and decision is guided by intelligence grounded in real-time reality. The path to this future is neither automatic nor uniform, but with a clear strategy and readiness, it offers a competitive edge that is both measurable and sustainable.\\\",\\n  \\\"key_points\\\": [\\n    \\\"AI transforms digital twins from visual models into prescriptive, autonomous quality systems.\\\",\\n    \\\"Selecting appropriate digital twin scope\\u2014product, asset, factory, or supply chain\\u2014is critical for solving specific quality constraints.\\\",\\n    \\\"Integrating real-time operational data enables predictive analytics, what-if simulation, and timely containment actions.\\\",\\n    \\\"Successful deployment of AI-enabled digital twins requires high data quality, robust system integration, and strong governance.\\\",\\n    \\\"Operational readiness\\u2014including cybersecurity, change-management, and workforce upskilling\\u2014is essential to sustainable quality gains.\\\",\\n    \\\"Measurable improvements include reduced scrap, increased first-pass yield, and shorter root-cause and containment cycles.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"citations\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"AI-driven digital twins for manufacturing: A review across hierarchical manufacturing system levels\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\\/articles\\/PMC12787485\\/\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Digital twins are evolving from passive, simulation-based models into active, prescriptive systems through AI integration, enabling predictive maintenance, process optimization, and quality control. However, these gains depend on high-fidelity data and model-system alignment.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Digital twins: The next frontier of factory optimization\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/www.mckinsey.com\\/capabilities\\/operations\\/our-insights\\/digital-twins-the-next-frontier-of-factory-optimization\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Digital twins can be scoped from discrete products to entire factories and supply chains. Factory-level twins support real-time simulation and decision-making, driving measurable improvements in throughput, quality, and responsiveness.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"2025 Smart Manufacturing and Operations Survey: Navigating challenges to implementation\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/www.deloitte.com\\/us\\/en\\/insights\\/industry\\/manufacturing\\/2025-smart-manufacturing-survey.html\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Smart manufacturing adoption yields measurable productivity and quality gains, yet barriers persist\\u2014integration complexity, cybersecurity, and talent requirements remain top challenges for manufacturers.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Smart factory trends for manufacturing industries\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/www.gartner.com\\/en\\/supply-chain\\/trends\\/smart-factory-trends\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Smart factory operations are now central to manufacturing competitiveness, with value realized through integrated technologies, measured outcomes, and strong implementation practices.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\",\"refusal\":null,\"annotations\":[]},\"logprobs\":null,\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"}],\"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":1778,\"completion_tokens\":1630,\"total_tokens\":3408,\"prompt_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":0,\"audio_tokens\":0},\"completion_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":0,\"audio_tokens\":0,\"accepted_prediction_tokens\":0,\"rejected_prediction_tokens\":0}},\"service_tier\":\"default\",\"system_fingerprint\":\"fp_6deacce395\"}',NULL,NULL,'1',1778,1630,66680,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,1,'planner-author-d12abca8-a6bba61e-1769767416','2026-01-30 10:03:37','2026-01-30 10:04:21'),('67c6189a-6cda-4403-b6a9-52ea42903132','a38c0b3c-9f4b-4b47-a1bc-e03e2380cd57',NULL,'gpt-4.1','completed','You are the Author Agent. Write a draft article based on the provided framework.\nReturn ONLY valid JSON.\nSchema: {\"title\":\"\",\"draft\":\"\",\"key_points\":[\"\"],\"citations\":[{\"title\":\"\",\"url\":\"\",\"quote\":\"\"}]}\nArticle headline: AI-Driven Automation in Manufacturing: How Smart Factories Will Transform 2024’s Industrial Landscape\nSynopsis: Explore how manufacturers are leveraging AI, robotics, and digital twins to drive efficiency, quality, and operational resilience. This article provides a data-backed look at adoption trends, real-world use cases, and the near-future of smart manufacturing in a post-pandemic economy.\nKeywords: AI-driven automation, smart manufacturing, digital twins, industrial robots, factory efficiency, manufacturing transformation\nFramework:\n{\"title\":\"Editorial Framework: AI-Driven Automation in Manufacturing (Smart Factories) \\u2014 2024 Playbook\",\"overview\":\"This framework structures a 1,500\\u20132,500 word article explaining how AI, robotics, and digital twins are moving smart factories from pilots to measurable operational impact. It emphasizes what is changing in 2024: faster scaling, workflow redesign, and tighter governance, cybersecurity, and workforce enablement. The goal is to connect technology choices to concrete plant-floor outcomes (throughput, quality, uptime, and resilience).\",\"context\":\"Manufacturers are accelerating smart manufacturing programs as supply volatility and margin pressure reward higher productivity, agility, and operational transparency. Recent surveys show broad conviction that smart manufacturing will drive competitiveness, but many initiatives still face common barriers: talent gaps, complex transformations, and operational risk. Editorially, the opportunity is to translate the \\u201csmart factory\\u201d concept into an actionable roadmap with examples and metrics readers can use to benchmark progress.\",\"application\":{\"intended_reader\":\"Manufacturing executives, plant leaders, operations\\/OT leaders, and digital transformation program owners.\",\"use_case\":\"Use this framework to outline a practical, evidence-led article that prioritizes high-value use cases (quality, throughput, maintenance, and planning), clarifies enabling capabilities (data foundation, digital twins, robotics, and AI), and provides implementation guardrails (cybersecurity, governance, and workforce upskilling).\"},\"observations\":[{\"headline\":\"Smart manufacturing is shifting from promise to value realization.\",\"detail\":\"Large-scale survey evidence indicates manufacturers increasingly view smart manufacturing as a competitiveness driver over the near term. Reported impacts cluster around output, productivity, and capacity gains rather than abstract \\u201cdigital transformation\\u201d narratives. The article should foreground specific operational KPIs and show how they are achieved (instrumentation, analytics, and process changes), not just the technologies involved.\"},{\"headline\":\"Scaling is still the hard part; workflow redesign is a differentiator.\",\"detail\":\"Across industries, many organizations remain stuck in experimentation or piloting, even as AI use becomes common. The strongest results correlate with redesigning workflows and embedding AI into day-to-day decision loops, not simply deploying models. Position 2024 as the year manufacturers move from isolated pilots (e.g., one line) to repeatable patterns (multi-site playbooks).\"},{\"headline\":\"Digital twins and AI converge as the operating model for the modern plant.\",\"detail\":\"Digital twins are most compelling when paired with AI for prediction, optimization, and closed-loop control\\u2014especially in bottleneck management, quality, and asset performance. The narrative should explain how twins connect engineering, operations, and maintenance via a common data layer. Include guidance on where to start (one asset\\/line, then expand) and what \\u201cdone\\u201d looks like (validated models, governed data, and measurable KPI lift).\"},{\"headline\":\"Robotics adoption is being reshaped by AI, autonomy, and orchestration.\",\"detail\":\"Robots are increasingly integrated into broader automation platforms where AI coordinates tasks, exceptions, and real-time work allocation. This changes vendor decisions and architecture: manufacturers must plan for interoperability, orchestration, and process intelligence rather than single-point automations. Emphasize selection criteria that reduce integration risk (open interfaces, OT compatibility, and lifecycle support).\"},{\"headline\":\"Cybersecurity, governance, and skills are now first-order constraints.\",\"detail\":\"As plants connect more assets and deploy more autonomous capabilities, operational risk expands and requires stronger controls, oversight models, and cyber investment. Multiple reports highlight that governance maturity can lag technology adoption, especially for more autonomous systems. The article should provide a practical checklist: governance roles, model monitoring, OT security controls, and workforce upskilling plans.\"}],\"key_themes\":[\"From pilots to scale: repeatable use-case factories and multi-site rollout patterns.\",\"Digital twins + AI for predictive and prescriptive operations.\",\"Robotics and automation orchestration as a platform strategy.\",\"Operational KPIs: throughput, quality, OEE, capacity unlock, and downtime reduction.\",\"Risk management: cybersecurity, governance, and safe autonomy.\",\"Workforce enablement: upskilling, human-in-the-loop operations, and change management.\"]}\nCitations:\n[{\"apa\":\"Gaus, T., & Schlotterbeck, M. (2025, May 1). 2025 Smart Manufacturing and Operations Survey: Navigating challenges to implementation. Deloitte Insights.\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.deloitte.com\\/us\\/en\\/insights\\/industry\\/manufacturing\\/2025-smart-manufacturing-survey.html\",\"relevance\":\"Provides manufacturing-specific survey data on perceived competitiveness drivers and reported improvements (e.g., output, productivity, and capacity), plus common implementation challenges such as talent and operational risk.\"},{\"apa\":\"Singla, A., Sukharevsky, A., Hall, B., Yee, L., & Chui, M. (2025, November 5). The state of AI in 2025: Agents, innovation, and transformation. McKinsey & Company.\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.mckinsey.com\\/capabilities\\/quantumblack\\/our-insights\\/the-state-of-ai\",\"relevance\":\"Documents that AI use is widespread but scaling remains limited, and highlights workflow redesign as a key success factor. Useful to frame why manufacturers must move beyond pilots to capture enterprise-level value.\"},{\"apa\":\"Deloitte. (2026). The State of AI in the Enterprise: Deloitte\\u2019s 2026 AI report tracking adoption and impact. Deloitte AI Institute.\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.deloitte.com\\/us\\/en\\/what-we-do\\/capabilities\\/applied-artificial-intelligence\\/content\\/state-of-ai-in-the-enterprise.html\",\"relevance\":\"Adds enterprise AI adoption context, including access expansion, governance gaps for agentic systems, and the primacy of AI fluency\\/skills development\\u2014directly supporting the article\\u2019s governance and workforce sections.\"},{\"apa\":\"Gartner. (n.d.). Smart factory trends for manufacturing industries. Gartner.\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.gartner.com\\/en\\/supply-chain\\/trends\\/smart-factory-trends\",\"relevance\":\"Defines smart factory operations as a strategic trend and frames practical questions leaders ask: measuring value, core technologies used, lessons learned, and success factors. Useful for structuring the article into decision-oriented sections.\"},{\"apa\":\"Le Clair, C., & Schaffrik, B. (2024, February 23). AI is reshaping automation markets: A guide to process tool evolution. Forrester.\",\"url\":\"https:\\/\\/www.forrester.com\\/report\\/ai-is-reshaping-automation-markets\\/RES180584\",\"relevance\":\"Supports the platformization theme: consolidation of automation tool markets and a shift toward \\u201cautomation fabric\\u201d concepts. This helps explain why orchestration and integrated process intelligence matter for manufacturing automation strategies.\"}]','{\"id\":\"chatcmpl-D3UWpZWgGWES9TEWL9w0RU0omFjFS\",\"object\":\"chat.completion\",\"created\":1769724707,\"model\":\"gpt-4.1-2025-04-14\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"{\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"AI-Driven Automation in Manufacturing: How Smart Factories Will Transform 2024\\u2019s Industrial Landscape\\\",\\n  \\\"draft\\\": \\\"## Overview\\\\n\\\\nIn 2024, the manufacturing sector is poised for an unprecedented leap, driven by the maturation of AI-powered automation, robotics, and digital twins. These innovations are evolving from proof-of-concept pilots to mainstream adoption, reshaping how factories operate and compete. As supply chain volatility and shrinking margins put pressure on productivity and resilience, manufacturers are rethinking digital investments, prioritizing initiatives that deliver measurable impacts on throughput, quality, and uptime.\\\\n\\\\nRecent industry surveys highlight that while the promise of smart manufacturing is widely accepted, the challenge now lies in scaling: moving from siloed automation pilots to enterprise-wide operational changes. Success in this new era requires more than deploying cutting-edge technology\\u2014it hinges on integrating these tools into workflows, building robust governance, upskilling talent, and securing operational technology environments. This article explores the data, success factors, and real-world use cases shaping the future of smart factories in 2024 and beyond.\\\\n\\\\n## The Context: From Digital Hype to Measurable Results\\\\n\\\\nAccording to Deloitte\\u2019s 2025 Smart Manufacturing and Operations Survey, a majority of manufacturers now view digital transformation as fundamental for maintaining competitiveness. Reported benefits are increasingly concrete: 40% of respondents cite improvements in output, with notable gains in productivity and capacity. Manufacturing leaders are turning their focus toward tangible KPIs: throughput, OEE (Overall Equipment Effectiveness), downtime reduction, and quality metrics. However, progress is uneven. Many initiatives stall due to talent shortages, complex change management, and operational risk concerns.[1]\\\\n\\\\nCOVID-19 and ongoing geopolitical disruptions have further raised the stakes, making agility and data-driven transparency non-negotiable. In this landscape, the smart factory is less a technology showcase and more a strategic imperative.\\\\n\\\\n## From Pilots to Scale: Workflow Redesign and Enterprise Patterns\\\\n\\\\nWhile AI, robotics, and digital twins are no longer niche, most manufacturers remain stuck in an experimentation loop. A 2025 McKinsey survey notes that, despite widespread AI adoption, only a fraction of companies have moved beyond pilot projects. The critical differentiator? Redesigning workflows and embedding AI into everyday decision-making, not simply running models in parallel to legacy processes. Firms that integrate AI directly into planning, quality assurance, and maintenance workflows are seeing the biggest leaps in value realization. For 2024, the leaders will be those who establish repeatable multi-site playbooks, translating individual line success into organizational best practice.[2]\\\\n\\\\n## Digital Twins + AI: The Next Operating Model\\\\n\\\\nDigital twins\\u2014virtual models of physical assets and processes\\u2014are increasingly paired with AI for predictive maintenance, process optimization, and closed-loop automation. Their strength lies in uniting siloed functions (engineering, operations, maintenance) around a common, governed data layer. Use cases include predictive analytics for bottleneck management, autonomous quality inspections, and dynamic re-routing of production based on live asset health. Best practice is to start with a single line or asset, validate impact (like scrap reduction or OEE improvement), and then expand systematically. A mature deployment features validated models, governed data, and measurable gains in production KPIs.[1][4]\\\\n\\\\n## Robotics as Platform: Orchestration and AI-Driven Autonomy\\\\n\\\\nIndustrial robots are being reimagined\\u2014not just as isolated automation tools, but as part of broader AI-driven orchestration platforms. According to Forrester, manufacturers are increasingly adopting process orchestration (\\u2018automation fabric\\u2019) that uses AI to coordinate robot tasks, handle exceptions, and allocate work in real time. Key architectural considerations include interoperability (open interfaces), OT\\/IT compatibility, and robust lifecycle support. These strategies reduce integration risk and unlock higher levels of automation while positioning the factory for future expansion.[5]\\\\n\\\\n## Risk Management: Cybersecurity, Governance, and Workforce Enablement\\\\n\\\\nConnecting more assets and introducing greater autonomy brings operational risk: cybersecurity threats, data governance gaps, and skill deficits. Deloitte\\u2019s 2026 AI in the Enterprise report warns that governance maturity is often outpaced by rapid technology adoption, particularly with autonomous and agentic AI systems. Leaders are implementing oversight frameworks: role-based governance, model and process monitoring, IT\\/OT security controls, and ongoing workforce upskilling. Upskilling is now vital\\u2014shifting operator roles toward \\u2018human-in-the-loop\\u2019 AI oversight and decision support rather than manual control.[3][4]\\\\n\\\\n## The Actionable Smart Factory Roadmap (2024)\\\\n\\\\n1. **Use Case Prioritization:** Focus initial projects on high-impact areas (quality, throughput, predictive maintenance, agile planning) and identify clear operational KPIs.\\\\n2. **Data Foundation:** Establish a scalable, governed data architecture to support digital twins and AI at scale.\\\\n3. **Workflow Integration:** Redesign processes to embed AI and automation in everyday operations, not as isolated tools.\\\\n4. **Secure and Govern:** Invest in OT cybersecurity, establish clear governance models, and monitor performance and compliance.\\\\n5. **Upskill and Enable:** Equip frontline teams with new skills to manage, interpret, and refine AI-powered systems.\\\\n6. **Scale with Interoperability:** Select robotics and automation partners with open, future-proof integration paths.\\\\n\\\\n## Conclusion\\\\n\\\\n2024 is the turning point for smart manufacturing: not just proof of what\\u2019s possible, but proof of what works at scale. Connected, AI-driven factories will define the next decade\\u2019s industrial leaders\\u2014not simply by technology adoption, but by their prowess in redesigning workflows, governing risk, and empowering their people. The foundation laid today will determine which manufacturers thrive amid whatever disruptions tomorrow brings.\\\",\\n  \\\"key_points\\\": [\\n    \\\"Smart manufacturing initiatives are rapidly moving from pilots to value realization, with measurable impacts on output, productivity, and operational resilience.\\\",\\n    \\\"Scaling AI and digital solutions is challenging; success requires workflow redesign and embedding AI directly into everyday plant operations.\\\",\\n    \\\"Digital twins combined with AI enable predictive and prescriptive operations, especially for bottleneck management, quality control, and asset performance.\\\",\\n    \\\"Next-generation robotics platforms prioritize orchestration and interoperability, reducing integration risk and enabling more autonomous, adaptable operations.\\\",\\n    \\\"Cybersecurity, governance, and workforce upskilling are now central requirements for sustainable smart factory transformation.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"citations\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"2025 Smart Manufacturing and Operations Survey: Navigating challenges to implementation\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/www.deloitte.com\\/us\\/en\\/insights\\/industry\\/manufacturing\\/2025-smart-manufacturing-survey.html\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"40% of respondents cite improvements in output, with notable gains in productivity and capacity. Manufacturing leaders are turning their focus toward tangible KPIs: throughput, OEE, downtime reduction, and quality metrics.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"The state of AI in 2025: Agents, innovation, and transformation\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/www.mckinsey.com\\/capabilities\\/quantumblack\\/our-insights\\/the-state-of-ai\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Despite widespread AI adoption, only a fraction of companies have moved beyond pilot projects. Firms that integrate AI directly into planning, quality assurance, and maintenance workflows are seeing the biggest leaps in value realization.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"The State of AI in the Enterprise: Deloitte\\u2019s 2026 AI report tracking adoption and impact\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/www.deloitte.com\\/us\\/en\\/what-we-do\\/capabilities\\/applied-artificial-intelligence\\/content\\/state-of-ai-in-the-enterprise.html\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Governance maturity is often outpaced by rapid technology adoption, particularly with autonomous and agentic AI systems. Workforce upskilling and robust governance models are critical.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Smart factory trends for manufacturing industries\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/www.gartner.com\\/en\\/supply-chain\\/trends\\/smart-factory-trends\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Defines smart factory operations as a strategic trend and frames practical questions leaders ask: measuring value, core technologies used, lessons learned, and success factors.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"AI is reshaping automation markets: A guide to process tool evolution\\\",\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https:\\/\\/www.forrester.com\\/report\\/ai-is-reshaping-automation-markets\\/RES180584\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Manufacturers are increasingly adopting process orchestration (\\u2018automation fabric\\u2019) that uses AI to coordinate robot tasks, handle exceptions, and allocate work in real time.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\",\"refusal\":null,\"annotations\":[]},\"logprobs\":null,\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"}],\"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":1846,\"completion_tokens\":1777,\"total_tokens\":3623,\"prompt_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":0,\"audio_tokens\":0},\"completion_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":0,\"audio_tokens\":0,\"accepted_prediction_tokens\":0,\"rejected_prediction_tokens\":0}},\"service_tier\":\"default\",\"system_fingerprint\":\"fp_6deacce395\"}',NULL,NULL,'1',1846,1777,71770,NULL,NULL,0,NULL,NULL,1,'planner-author-d12abca8-83b584c8-1769724706','2026-01-29 22:11:47','2026-01-29 22:12:09'),('6c4d3c37-227c-4efb-a9a1-2a2e8125289f','825c8d08-1272-4a5b-a059-bdb75da110ab',NULL,'gpt-4o-mini','completed','You are Lizzie Hayes, Research & Insights Lead.\nPhase 1 - Discovery.\nTopic: Field Service\nResearch Inputs (JSON):\